  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nThrough Monday: As anticipated previously, storms have once again\ndeveloped with strong daytime heating and are mainly focused over\nthe southeast two thirds of the area. A favorable environment for\nmicrobursts with vertical totals near 26 deg C and DCAPE near \n1500 j/kg is supporting a few intense storms with strong to\nmarginally severe wind gusts. This storm threat should continue \nto the early evening hours. The storm threat for Monday should\nshift a little farther south and west with coverage a little less.\nOtherwise, dangerous heat continues to be the primary concern, \nand the heat advisory was extended to Monday and expanded to \ninclude northeast portions of the forecast area. /EC/\n\nTuesday through Sunday: The subtropical high will strengthen over\nour region as we go later into the week with the center of the \nhigh just to our north. This will result in low level flow \nbecoming more easterly over the mid to latter portions of the \nweek, which will eventually usher in a relatively drier airmass, \nespecially by Thursday. This airmass will still support hot days, \nbut with dewpoints dropping into 60s and perhaps the 50s, this \nwill notably reduce heat stress danger through the end of the \nweek. While days will still be relatively warm, mornings will see \ntemps dipping into the 60s more often across much of the area. \nDeep layer moisture will begin to increase again around Friday \ninto Saturday, with returning potential for diurnal convection \neach day. /DL/EC/",
